# MarketHub — environment template
#
# Copy to `.env` and fill in. `.env` is gitignored; this file is not, so it must
# never contain a real secret.
#
# Save `.env` as UTF-8 WITHOUT a byte order mark. Nitro parses it with
# `node:util.parseEnv`, which keeps a BOM as part of the first key name, so a
# file saved as "UTF-8 with BOM" turns the first variable into an unreadable
# `\uFEFFNAME` and it reads back as unset with no error anywhere. Several
# Windows editors add the BOM by default. See src/lib/server/env.ts.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Server data store
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where the JSON store lives. Defaults to ./data/markethub.json.
# Point this at a mounted volume in a deployment so the file is not inside the
# deployed bundle. The directory `data/` is gitignored because the file holds
# scrypt password hashes and live session ids.
# MH_DATA_FILE=/var/lib/markethub/markethub.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Seeded demo accounts
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Password given to every seeded sample account (the 8 sellers and 6 shoppers
# from src/lib/data.ts). Defaults to Demo@1234 when unset.
#
# This is only acceptable because every seeded account is fictional sample
# data. Do not reuse this value for anything real.
# MH_SEED_PASSWORD=Demo@1234

# The admin account is created ONLY if this is set, and there is no default.
# That is deliberate: a privileged account must never exist with a guessable
# password just because someone booted the app. Leave unset and no admin
# account is created at all.
# MH_ADMIN_PASSWORD=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hubby AI assistant (pre-existing, optional)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without a key the assistant falls back to offline keyword matching.
#
# Keys issued by Google AI Studio since May 2026 are "auth keys" and start with
# `AQ.` — the older `AIza` standard keys are no longer accepted. Sent as the
# `x-goog-api-key` header. If the server log shows "Gemini rejected
# GEMINI_API_KEY", the key is wrong, revoked, or not permitted to call the
# Generative Language API; issue a new one rather than retrying.
# GEMINI_API_KEY=
#
# Free-tier quota is metered per project PER MODEL, and it is small: Google
# returned a limit of 20 generate_content requests/day for gemini-3.8-flash,
# with a ~15h retry delay once spent. A demo session can exhaust that in a
# handful of questions, after which Hubby serves the offline matcher and the
# "busy" notice.
#
# Because the bucket is per model, pointing GEMINI_MODEL at a different model
# gives a fresh daily allowance. Confirmed available on this project include
# gemini-2.5-flash, gemini-3.5-flash, gemini-3.7-flash and gemini-flash-latest.
# For anything beyond a demo, enable billing instead of model-hopping.
# GEMINI_MODEL=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runtime
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOT required for cookie security. Vite inlines `process.env.NODE_ENV` as
# "production" when building, so the built server always sets the `Secure` flag
# on the session cookie regardless of whether this is set at runtime. Verified
# directly against the production bundle.
#
# `vite dev` sets it to "development", which is what keeps the Secure flag off
# for local http development.
# NODE_ENV=production

# Port to listen on. Most hosts inject this automatically. Defaults to 3000.
# PORT=3000
